 to do is get to the next  meeting and you just need somebody to  trust that this is going to be good it's  hard man  so venturing past the studio's own  borders allowed Santa Monica to adapt to  an ever evolving development scene they  could learn from Sony's other first  party teams choosing to keep their  borders open so as not to suffocate  behind their own walls but Santa Monica  also looked to developers outside of  Sony's portfolio not just current  projects but games that have been around  for decades inspiring a long line of  iterations in their wake we were  influenced by all the games around this  you know we came to the table with our  love of Metroid Castlevania Resident  Evil Onimusha revenue before came out  when we were developing I think - so it  was like the transition between 1 &amp; 2  resi before came out and you know we  follow a lot of the the Japanese game  developers especially in the early years  mostly because we admired all the work  that they had done and there were  several Japanese game developers who  said you know Western universe can't  make a good action game so we were like  we're gonna take you guys on and I think  that love of their games just continued  past that point anyone resin Evil 4 came  out that was as cliched is it that was a  game changer right that was this sense  that they had said the director of NIDA  had said you can't have a controllable  camera and have horror you'll never get  scares it's not possible  and then he totally figured it out and  in a spectacularly amazing way right and  that was inspirational I think everybody  played that game and everybody was  coming into the office and everybody was  talking about it you know interestingly  at the time it didn't inspire us I think  reinvent anything but little did I know  it would inspire later that sense of  bravery and it took seven to come out  that really cemented they're not afraid  to go back and re-examine everything and  that ability to do that and to 